## Overview

This project covers migrating an existing home lab environment — a physical Hyper-V host — over to Proxmox VE, without losing any of the work already built on top of it, including a live Windows Server failover cluster with shared iSCSI storage. The goal was to change hypervisors without starting from scratch, and to understand what actually has to be verified after a migration like this to trust that everything still works the way it did before.

## Why Migrate

The original lab was built on a physical Hyper-V host (FUSION-DT). Moving to Proxmox VE was driven by wanting more flexibility for future lab builds — nested virtualization, easier snapshotting, and a platform better suited to running mixed Windows/Linux lab environments (like the Linux-based iSCSI targets used elsewhere in the lab) side by side.

## Migration Approach

This was effectively a **P2V (Physical-to-Virtual) migration** — moving a workload running on physical hardware into a virtualized environment on the new host. **Veeam** was used as the migration tool, chosen for its ability to handle the transfer cleanly without requiring a full manual rebuild of every VM from scratch.

The migration process followed a few key stages:

1. **Pre-migration inventory** — documenting exactly what was running on the source Hyper-V host, including the failover cluster nodes, their storage dependencies (the iSCSI shared disk), and any networking configuration that would need to carry over
2. **Migration execution** — using Veeam to move the VMs from the Hyper-V source to the Proxmox destination
3. **Post-migration validation** — this was the critical step, and where most of the real work happened

## Post-Migration Validation

Moving VMs between hypervisors isn't the hard part — confirming everything still actually *works* afterward is. A few things specifically needed re-validation:

- **Cluster health** — since the migrated environment included a Windows Server failover cluster (see the [Failover Cluster]({{ site.url }}/portfolio/failover-cluster-lab) project), cluster validation had to be re-run post-migration to confirm nothing about the underlying virtual hardware change broke cluster communication or storage access
- **SCSI-3 Persistent Reservation behavior** — this was a particular point of attention, since PR validation had already caused problems once during the original cluster build (see the linked project for that story). Re-confirming this behavior specifically after a hypervisor change, rather than assuming it would "just work" because it worked before, avoided any surprises
- **Networking** — confirming virtual NICs, VLANs, and any static IP/network configuration carried over correctly on the new virtual hardware layer under Proxmox

## Lessons Learned

- **A migration isn't done when the VM boots — it's done when everything dependent on that VM is re-validated.** For a standalone VM, that might just mean confirming the OS boots and services start. For something like a cluster, it means re-testing the specific mechanisms (like SCSI-3 PR) that could be sensitive to underlying hardware changes.
- **Documenting the source environment before migrating matters.** Knowing exactly what dependencies existed (shared storage, specific network config) made it possible to know what to check afterward, rather than discovering gaps reactively.
- **Choosing the right migration tool saves significant time.** Using Veeam instead of manually rebuilding every VM meant the migration itself was fast — the real time investment went into validation, which is exactly where it should go.

